Z||000000||mwbackus||02-06-2000||02:21 AM||||Fiero Store price is a bit scary for this item.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   Anyone know if it is still available from GM, or what the OEM part number is (from the 22p)?<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||02-06-2000||03:03 AM||||I looked it up in my 22P manual and didn't find a solenoid. Is it the part that the vacuum line connects to on the very top? If so the part # is: 17110813. They call it a valve and is for a V6 ( you have a Formula right?). Mine has never gone out so I know nothing about it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||02-06-2000||11:24 AM||||Close, thats the number for the EGR valve. Its the solenoid that operates the EGR valve that I am interested in.  It is located just below the coolant filler neck, looks like this:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.secureleadercom.com/fierostore/images/68324.GIF"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||02-06-2000||02:38 PM||||10038324||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||02-06-2000||06:34 PM||||I bought one, back in the summer. Was $102(!) at the dealer. Fixed my code 32 problem. Until last week, that is.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||02-06-2000||06:35 PM||||Thanks SCCA, you have been of great assistance!  I guess I will end up buying a used one.  I removed mine and dismantled it for inspection.  The terminals inside the solenoid itself were totally covered with goopy green colored corrosion (yuk), and one wire was barely making contact.  The seals were also leaking slightly.  I believe this leak was causing a slight miss, although the idle rpm was still normal (~900rpm).  Hopefully I will see some improvments upon replacement.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Mike<P>P.S.<BR>The unit appeared to be in perfectly good condition externally, but since I knew it had to be original equip. I thought it best to inspect it.  Glad I did!<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 02-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||02-06-2000||09:06 PM||||The billions of the Celebrity MPFI's in the junkyards have the same one.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Linx||02-06-2000||11:21 PM||||Consider sealing your new solenoid with rtv before you install it.<P>I examined mine a month after I'd replaced it, and it was already showing signs of corrosion.  This thing just aint designed well enough to be exposed to the elements.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mwbackus||02-07-2000||12:26 AM||||Thats a VERY good idea Linx! I was amazed at the amount of corrosion found inside it.  The vacuum sensor switch part looked ok, which is probably why it never set a code.  I am positive that the solenoid itself was leaking slight vacuum and sticking, very likely responsible for some unexplained performance problems I have been trying to track down.  I guess it best to just bite the bullet and buy a new one. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000009||wwh0001||02-07-2000||11:19 AM||||I just picked up the solenoid ( EGR SWITCH) you are talking about from Auto Zone, cost was bout $30.00||||reg||1
Z||000010||mwbackus||02-07-2000||10:01 PM||||The autozone here does not offer this item.  Same story with checker and napa.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>I found that a small o-ring on the tube end of the solenoid had deteriorated (hard and cracked) to a point that it was obviously leaking a slight amount of vacuum.  It is interesting to note that the leak tests I have done in the past (ie.. starting fluid, WD40, etc.) did not reveal this.  I did notice a very slight vacuum on the line to the EGR valve during idle (after driving around one day).<BR> <BR>I determined that it may be possible that leakage from this bad o-ring has resulted in a slight opening of the EGR valve at idle and WOT, thus causing surging at cruise speed, rough idle, etc..  To verify this, I plugged the vacuum connection (where the two lines connect to the solenoid) and left electrical plug off(eg. no solenoid present..anyway).<P>I was suprised that no codes set due to this, and that scans of the ECM during operation indicated a normal EGR demand(%).<BR>The performance problems were absent, so it is quite likely that this leaky EGR solenoid was responsible. <BR>Note : the EGR valve was recently replaced with a new AC-Delco unit. <P><BR>Mike<P>P.S.   Sorry for the long drawl, but perhaps this info will help those with similar problems?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tom88gt||02-11-2000||01:07 PM||||No luck finding the solenoid here either.<P>wwh0001....can you tell me where this Auto Zone is?  The one here don't carry it.  Maybe you can sell solenoids to us who aren't blessed with a fully stocked Auto Zone <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||5
Z||000012||batboy||02-11-2000||08:53 PM||||Most parts stores will order stuff from their warehouse for you if you request them to. Usually only takes a couple days at most. I've done this many times in the past.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mwbackus||02-11-2000||09:52 PM||||The problem is that the parts stores in question DO NOT stock this item (ie.. it is not available at all).  I think that wwh0001 was probably confused, since Autozone uses a national database for their stock lookups.  There are many solenoids of this type used on fuel injected GM's, but they are all slightly different than the Fiero V6 design.  I located one that was similar to the Fiero's (ie.. had 3 vacuum attachment ponts on the front). the solenoid itself was mounted backwards, and filter was a different design. It was possible to replace the filter with that of the Fiero's, and rearrange the components so that it was exactly the same.  The electrical connector was the same on both units.  I believe it was from an 89 Pontiac 6000, it looked almost new, and it works perfectly.  Cost : $4.10 at pick&pull.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000014||2birds||02-11-2000||10:42 PM||||I got a little overzealous at the car wash and got a code 32 for my trouble, back in 1994.  The guy at the NAPA store gave me the deer-in-the-headlights look when I tried to buy one from him.  The Pontiac dealer got around $95 from me for my new EGR vacuum solenoid.   Thanks to terryk for the interchange info if this ever happens again. ||||reg||1
